# GitHub & Git strategy (agent must follow)

**SSOT docs (read when unsure):** `docs/BRANCHING_STRATEGY.md`, `docs/VERSIONING_POLICY.md`, `docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md`

Maintafox is a **tightly integrated product**. The product owner runs the whole app daily from **`develop`**. A finished feature that is not on `develop` is **unfinished**.

The product owner is not a programmer. After major or fundamental work (or a coherent series of related changes), **you must commit, push, verify, and merge into `develop`** unless they explicitly ask to keep work isolated.

## Daily integration model (`develop` first)

```
feature|fix|chore (short-lived) → develop (PR, merge promptly after verify)
develop → staging → main (PR)
hotfix from release tag on main → PR to main + PR to develop
```

1. **`develop` is the daily integration branch.** Merge finished features **immediately after verification**. Day-to-day continuation and “run the app” assume **`develop`**.
2. **Feature branches are temporary only.** Create them for **large or risky** changes that may temporarily break the app. Lifetime: **hours or a few days**, not weeks. After `cargo check`, `pnpm typecheck`, and `pnpm rbac:check` when RBAC changed → merge to `develop` → **delete/archive** the branch.
3. **Never leave completed features in stashes or long-lived feature branches.**
4. **Stash is emergency storage only** — never SSOT.
5. **Do not intentionally keep finished features separated** from `develop` unless the owner explicitly asks.
6. **Before creating a branch:** search branches, search stashes, verify work is not already present, continue existing work instead of duplicating.
7. **`cleanup/*` / reconstruction are one-time maintenance only** — never the development base.

- Work only on `feature/*`, `fix/*`, `chore/*`, or `hotfix/*` — never commit directly to `main`, `staging`, or `develop`.
- If detached/wrong branch: switch to the correct short-lived branch **before** committing.
- Prefer branching from **updated `develop`**, not from cleanup or stale feature tips.

## When to commit and push (timing)

Do this automatically when any of these are true:

1. A feature/fix/chore slice is **functionally complete** (builds, hooks pass, intent delivered).
2. A **series of related edits** forms one logical unit (e.g. DI triage + migrations + i18n).
3. Work is **risky to lose** (schema, IPC, auth, migrations, multi-file refactors).
4. The user asks to save/push progress, or ends a major task without an open “hold commits” request.

Then: **push → open/update PR to `develop` → merge after verification** (do not park finished work).

Do **not** spam micro-commits for every tiny edit mid-debug; batch into one conventional commit per logical unit.

### Session commit discipline (mandatory)

**No feature may remain uncommitted for more than one logical work session.**

When stable: short-lived branch (if needed) → commit → push → verify → **merge to `develop`** → delete the temporary branch.

Do **not** accumulate unrelated work across modules in one working tree. Do **not** work for days/weeks with only a dirty tree or stash.

## One branch = one feature

- Each `feature/*` owns **one** coherent slice.
- Do not pile unrelated recoveries or modules onto one branch.

## No duplicate branches (search first)

Before creating any feature/fix/chore branch:

1. Search local and remote branches (`git branch -a`).
2. Search stashes (`git stash list`).
3. Confirm the work is not already on `develop` or another tip.
4. If a matching branch/stash exists → **continue / restore there**.
5. If uncertain → **ask before branching**.

## Temporary cleanup / reconstruction

- One-time maintenance only — **never** the daily base.
- Merge to `develop` promptly, then archive or delete.
- New work after that lands from **`develop`**, not from cleanup.
- Do not leave product features living only as `cleanup → feature → feature` away from `develop`.

## Stashes are temporary only (mandatory)

**A stash must never become the only copy (SSOT) of product code.**

Before dropping, popping, or replacing any stash, verify every unique change is reachable from a named ref (preferably already merged or about to merge to `develop`).

If unique code exists only in the stash:

1. Short-lived feature branch.
2. Restore → commit → push.
3. Verify → **merge to `develop`** (unless owner asks to isolate).
4. Then drop the stash.

Never rely on dangling Git objects as backup.

### Safe stash removal

Never execute `git stash drop` or `git stash clear` until the agent explicitly reports:

```text
SAFE TO DELETE
```

Including: reachability verified; no unique product code only in the stash; recovery no longer required.

## Reachability check (mandatory)

Before deleting any branch, stash, or temporary ref, verify unique commits/blobs are on a permanent branch (usually `develop`) or intentional archive. Preserve first if not.

## Pre-reconstruction / pre-recovery snapshot (mandatory)

Before **any** reconstruction or recovery operation, run and **save**:

```bash
git status
git branch --all
git stash list
git log --graph --decorate --oneline --all -50
git tag
```

Persist under `docs/engineering/git-snapshots/` (e.g. `YYYYMMDD-HHMM-pre-recovery.md`) and/or the session report. Do not start destructive recovery until saved.

## Recovery branches

If recovered work is unrelated to current WIP:

- Do **not** merge it into the current WIP branch.
- Dedicated short-lived feature branch → exact restore → commit (no opportunistic refactor).
- Verify → **merge to `develop`** unless the owner explicitly asks to keep it isolated → delete the recovery branch.

Recovered features must become first-class history on **`develop`**, not stash leftovers.

## Branch health check

After every sprint, and **before starting any new sprint**, generate a **Branch Health Report**:

| Section | Include |
|---------|---------|
| Active feature branches | Purpose + tip; **finished but not on `develop`?** |
| Temporary integration | `cleanup/*` — merge then delete |
| Scratch | `safety/*`, `compare/*`, `recovery/*` — local only |
| Stale | remote `[gone]`, abandoned weeks-old features |
| Merge order | What should land on `develop` next |
| Dependency graph | Nothing should stay stacked on cleanup |

Recommend merges into `develop` and branches that should disappear.

## Repository inventory

After every major feature or sprint: inventory of branches (by status), dangling commits, `git gc` risk, and **any finished work still missing from `develop`**.

## Commit message style (unified conventional)

```text
type(scope): short imperative summary

- optional body bullets: why / what modules
```

| type | use for |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | new capability |
| `fix` | bug fix |
| `chore` | gitignore, deps, tooling, non-app docs |
| `docs` | documentation only |
| `refactor` | no behavior change |
| `test` | tests only |
| `perf` | performance |

- Scope = module area: `di`, `org`, `auth`, `admin`, `wo`, `gitignore`, `phase5`, `git`, etc.
- Subject: imperative, ≤ ~72 chars, no trailing period.

## Safe commit procedure (mandatory checklist)

1. `git status` + `git diff` + `git log -5` (style check).
2. Stage **only source of truth**: app code, migrations, shared IPC, docs intended for the change.
3. **Never stage:** secrets, `node_modules/`, `src-tauri/target/`, `src-tauri/target-*`, `target-check*`, build caches, IDE junk, large binaries unless explicitly requested.
4. If huge untracked `target*` trees appear: **do not** `git add -A`; stage paths explicitly.
5. Commit with HEREDOC/`-m` multi-line conventional message (PowerShell-safe on Windows).
6. If pre-commit fails: **fix** and create a **new** commit — never `--no-verify` except exact-recovery below; never amend a failed commit.
7. `git push -u origin HEAD`. **No force-push** to protected branches.
8. Open/update PR to **`develop`**; after verification, **merge promptly** and delete the temporary branch.
9. Report commit SHA + branch + PR + merge result.

### Exact-recovery `--no-verify` exception

Allowed **only if all** are true: user requested exact/byte-identical recovery; lint/format would destroy equality; reason documented in commit message and/or final report. Otherwise forbidden.

## Branch naming

Allowed: `feature/p{N}-sp{NN}-{slug}`, `fix/{issue-id}-{slug}`, `chore/{slug}`, `hotfix/{version}-{slug}`.

`cleanup/*` allowed only as one-time maintenance. Scratch (`safety/*`, `compare/*`, `recovery/*`, `backup-*`) is **local only** — never push, never name `v*`.

After merge to `develop`, **delete** the temporary branch (optional short local archive). Do not keep finished features for weeks.

## Porting fixes

Reuse existing fixes — never re-implement. Prefer `git cherry-pick -x`. If only in a stash: restore to a branch, commit, merge to `develop`. Never merge an old branch wholesale just to grab one fix.

## Pull requests

- One PR per temporary branch toward **`develop`**. Update existing PR — no duplicates.
- Title format from `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` when it is a sprint feature; chores/docs may use `[chore] …`.
- Labels only from `.github/labels.yml`.
- Never bypass required status checks; fix forward on red CI.
- Goal of a feature PR: **merge into `develop` soon**, not linger open for weeks.

## Version and release management

**Every release must be reproducible.** Only merge-tested commits on the promotion path may be version-tagged. Never tag `cleanup/*`, reconstruction, feature tips, or scratch refs. Tags only on `main` after `develop` → `staging` → `main`.

Follow `docs/VERSIONING_POLICY.md` and `docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md` during explicit release tasks.

## Hard forbidden actions

| Action | Status |
|--------|--------|
| Direct commit to `main` / `staging` / `develop` | Forbidden |
| Force-push protected branches | Never |
| Amend/rebase **pushed** shared commits | Forbidden |
| `--no-verify` / skip hooks | Forbidden except exact-recovery |
| Stash as SSOT / finished work only in stash | Forbidden |
| `git stash drop` / `clear` without `SAFE TO DELETE` | Forbidden |
| Long-lived feature branches for finished work | Forbidden — merge to `develop` |
| Keeping finished features off `develop` without explicit owner request | Forbidden |
| Using `cleanup/*` as daily development base | Forbidden |
| Duplicate branch for same feature/sprint | Forbidden — continue or ask |
| Version tags off `main` / on cleanup or feature tips | Forbidden |
| Uncommitted stable work across sessions | Forbidden |
| Recovery without saved baseline snapshot | Forbidden |
| Re-implementing a fix that already exists elsewhere | Forbidden — cherry-pick |
| Merging unrelated recovery into current WIP | Forbidden — dedicated branch then to `develop` |

## End-of-task report (always)

- commit SHA + message; branch; PR; **merged to `develop`?**
- exclusions; ported commits; release-tag note if relevant
- finished work still **not** on `develop` (treat as defect)
- Branch Health / inventory after major sprints
- exact-recovery justification if hooks skipped

```text
❌ BAD — finished feature living weeks on feature/* or in stash; develop missing completed work
❌ BAD — cleanup as daily base; duplicate branches; drop stash without SAFE TO DELETE
✅ GOOD — verify → merge to develop promptly → delete temporary branch
✅ GOOD — run daily app from develop with all completed features present
```
